  "worked_solution": "In part 1, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=e+2\\ne 0.$\n\nApplying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}{x}^{3}+2}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2}{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{e+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
